WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
- We are back, and our
next guest is a powerhouse

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.950
in the world of true crime.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.200
Her podcast "Crime Junkie" gets more

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.073
than 30 million downloads a month.

00:00:11.073 --> 00:00:12.300
- [Crowd Member] Whoa.
(crowd exclaiming)

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.340
- Let that sink in. That's insane.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.700
It's one of many podcasts
in her growing media empire,

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.580
which will soon include her
very own Sirius channel.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.763
Let's welcome Ashley Flowers, everybody.

00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:27.763
(crowd clapping)
(bright energetic music)

00:00:39.747 --> 00:00:40.950
So we, little known fact,

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.480
literally were together yesterday,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.800
'cause we are channel buddies.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.060
- Yes.
- Yes, we're channel buddies.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.710
So we both have a Sirius channel now.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.730
- [Ashley] Yeah, old friends now.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.650
- Yes, so "Crime Junkies," the podcast.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.120
When did you feel like it, like, blew up?

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.140
When did you know that happened?

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:52.973
- Oh, my goodness.

00:00:52.973 --> 00:00:56.310
So I intended to make just,
like, this small local thing,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.410
and at the one year
anniversary, I'm, like,

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.550
in love with New Year's Eve.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.410
Like, it is my Christmas.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.930
And so I was like, "Let's
do a New Year's Eve party

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.400
for 'Crime Junkies' and, you know,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.110
we can have some fans come.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.670
It'll be great, we'll celebrate, you know,

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.560
having a year of the
podcast under our belt."

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.360
- Yeah.
- And all of a sudden,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.150
like, people from, like,
California and Georgia

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.770
were, like, buying tickets to this thing

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.860
and flying into Indiana on New Year's Eve.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.040
And I was like-
- Which does not happen,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:21.873
I don't feel like.

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:23.638
- [Ashley] It's not a
destination. (laughing)

00:01:23.638 --> 00:01:26.557
- It's not Vegas, you know
what I'm saying? Yeah, yeah.

00:01:26.557 --> 00:01:29.910
- So I was just like,
"Wow, this thing might be

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.775
bigger than I thought it was gonna be."

00:01:31.775 --> 00:01:33.870
And at the party that night,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.870
we got told that "Rolling Stone" named us

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.550
one of the top five True Crime podcasts.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.780
So it was, like, everything at once.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.203
And that was the moment
- What a beautiful thing.

00:01:41.203 --> 00:01:42.960
- Where I was like, "Oh,
my gosh, this is big."

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.640
- Oh, my gosh. Top five.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.291
I'd say you're the top. So how
did you get into true crime?

00:01:47.291 --> 00:01:50.880
- So my mom was like the OG crime junkie,

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:51.960
her mom before her.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.210
So when growing up,
like, it was Nancy Drew

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.380
and then Agatha Christie, like,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.090
all those good fiction things-
- Love Agatha Christie.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.170
- [Ashley] Same.
- Yeah.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.750
- And so at some point though, you know,

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.610
I realized that they're, like, real cases,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.270
and I would say it was when
the JonBenet case happened,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.460
because me and my best friend-
- You were young

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.293
to remember that.

00:02:09.293 --> 00:02:11.160
Yeah, yeah.
- Me and my best friend Brit,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.350
who I do the show with,
we have the same birthday,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.207
best friends since we were, like, born.

00:02:15.207 --> 00:02:18.330
And we were, like, tabloid
height when that happened.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.170
- Yeah, I was gonna say you were young.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.940
- We were young, and really I was almost,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.007
I was maybe just a year
or two younger than her,

00:02:23.007 --> 00:02:24.968
and that was the first time where it

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:27.150
was literally put in my face.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.764
That not only do these real things happen,

00:02:28.764 --> 00:02:31.560
but they happened to someone my age,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.050
someone like me and-
- Did that terrify you?

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.140
That seems terrifying.
- It probably should have.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.327
I mean, it was scary. I
definitely remember being scared.

00:02:39.327 --> 00:02:42.360
I obviously didn't, like, go research it

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.740
and, like, tell people about the case,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.202
but it's what got me interested.

00:02:45.202 --> 00:02:46.562
And as I got older and older,

00:02:46.562 --> 00:02:48.722
just started looking up more cases

00:02:48.722 --> 00:02:50.580
and wanting to talk about them

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.100
and wanting to figure out, like,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.240
where's my place in all of this?

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.700
Like, what can I do? Because
I felt so moved by the cases.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.520
- Yeah, so you, like,
you wanna solve cases.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.620
This is a big thing that
you are actually a part of,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.730
actually, you and your fans.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.170
- Yeah, I wanted to be a detective.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.280
Nobody's giving me a badge,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.878
so I had to go about it a different way.

00:03:06.878 --> 00:03:07.711
(audience laughing)

00:03:07.711 --> 00:03:09.156
- Nobody's giving me a badge. (laughing)

00:03:09.156 --> 00:03:11.041
I'm gonna get you your own badge.

00:03:11.041 --> 00:03:12.209
- [Ashley] Okay, deal.

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:13.890
- (laughing) That's what's
gonna happen for Christmas.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.960
No, that's incredible. So
explain the non-profit.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.400
It's called Season of Justice.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.590
- Yeah, so, you know, when
we started the podcast,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.450
for me it was all about, you know,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.262
if I'm taking and taking,
even when I was a consumer,

00:03:23.262 --> 00:03:26.130
if I'm gonna be, like,
consuming this voraciously,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.240
like, crime junkies do.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.560
Like, I can't stop.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.360
What can I do to actually,
like, make a difference?

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.220
And so it started, you
know, with volunteering.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.200
I volunteered for my local Crime Stoppers.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.437
It started with donating.

00:03:35.437 --> 00:03:38.130
Then I got to the point as
the podcast was getting bigger

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.230
at the same time we were getting

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.300
new investigative genealogy,
which was something

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.960
that nobody knew was possible,
nobody had budgeted for.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.700
And what I kept hearing
over and over again

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.070
from law enforcement agencies
and from families is, like,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.470
the only thing standing
in the way of this case

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.654
getting solved is money.

00:03:53.654 --> 00:03:55.838
And I was like, "I'll write you a check."

00:03:55.838 --> 00:03:57.720
And they were like, "We don't want a check

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.275
from a media company,
thank you very much."

00:03:59.275 --> 00:04:01.560
- And so, oh, they're not
allowed to, like, yeah.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.150
- It gets muddy. And
so I was like, "Okay."

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.310
So we went through all the work

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.180
to set up a separate entity
to fund this testing.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.310
So it's been in business or, you know,

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.980
the nonprofit's been
established since 2020.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.320
We've solved nine cold cases.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.359
11 Jane and John Does
have got their name back.

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:19.260
- Like, you're not just talking about it.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:20.543
You're actually helping people.

00:04:20.543 --> 00:04:21.390
(audience clapping)
It's really cool.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.310
I wanna talk to you
about, it's Odessa, Texas.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.740
That's where those two
fans, like, listeners

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.200
listening and then they
ended up being involved

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.580
with this case in Odessa, right?

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.420
- Right.
- So wait.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:31.380
Explain to everybody.
- Okay.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:32.910
So there was this case from back

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.120
in the '80s in Odessa, Texas
where this priest was murdered.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.725
And they initially thought that

00:04:38.725 --> 00:04:40.470
the last guy who was with him,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.000
they thought that he was responsible,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.042
couldn't really prove it.

00:04:43.042 --> 00:04:45.090
He ended up confessing.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.390
We know so much more about
false confessions now.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.860
And so he was imprisoned.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.230
And the wild part is he was imprisoned

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.200
even though he had receipts,
he had a speeding ticket,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.650
an actual government document
proving that he was 250 miles

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.540
away when the crime happened.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.760
He spent 20 years in prison,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.630
another 20 on parole as
a convicted murderer.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.100
And we, you know-
- Geez.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.630
- We tell this story being like, you know,

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.041
it's so clear to anyone
who looks at it now

00:05:12.041 --> 00:05:14.190
that it couldn't have been him.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.780
But it was thought for a long time

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.890
that nothing could be done,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.170
because a lot of the physical evidence

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.180
that could potentially, you
know, with new technology,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.400
prove that it was someone
else, had been lost.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.070
Everyone thought it was
gone, destroyed. Who knows?

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.290
So we tell this story,

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:33.063
and we get this email
from these two listeners.

00:05:33.063 --> 00:05:34.260
- Yeah.
- Out of nowhere.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.020
And they're like, "Hey, we heard it,

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.050
and we just happened to
be going to visit our dad,

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.650
who by the way, is the chief
of police in Odessa, Texas.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.510
And we tell him about this,

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.070
because, you know, we'd
never even heard of it.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:47.903
How can we not know about this?"

00:05:47.903 --> 00:05:49.650
- What a small world.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.140
Does he know about this?
Of course he doesn't.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.520
He wasn't there when it happened.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.740
He's, you know, got his workload.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.010
So they tell him, he goes and has his team

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:03.900
just take a look around,
see what you can find.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.630
They find the missing evidence.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.340
- [Crowd Member] Wow.
- And so all-

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.510
- That many years later?
That's incredible.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.360
- That many years later, they
found an old fingerprint card

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.970
from the crime scene,
which basically proved

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:18.543
that it wasn't James Reyos's
prints that were there.

00:06:18.543 --> 00:06:20.589
The prints got matched to other people.

00:06:20.589 --> 00:06:24.900
And this chief of police, I
mean, like, my hat off to him

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.720
doing the right thing, like,
no matter if it's hard,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.220
no matter what it means,

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.980
he got the Innocence
Project of Texas involved

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.190
to help James, like, get
this through the system.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.250
And on October 4th, 2023,

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.381
he was fully exonerated.

00:06:40.381 --> 00:06:42.404
(audience clapping)

00:06:42.404 --> 00:06:44.550
- It's just, like, I don't even know.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.010
It's such an amazing thing
but, like, what does that mean

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.110
for such loss in someone's life?

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.101
You know what I'm saying?

00:06:50.101 --> 00:06:51.480
Like-
- It was a long time, right?

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:54.120
And people think about,
"Oh, he was on parole."

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.446
You can't get certain jobs.

00:06:55.446 --> 00:06:57.990
You can't vote, you can't-
- People look at you

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.820
differently even sometimes, you know?

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:01.410
- But now that he's exonerated, I mean,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.100
not only does he get his
name back, his dignity back,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.470
but now he's eligible to be compensated

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.930
by the state of Texas who
wrongfully imprisoned him.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.280
- [Kelly] Wow.
- Yeah.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.880
- Okay, Ashley has never
talked to or met this couple,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.978
so we invited them to join us today-

00:07:16.978 --> 00:07:18.960
- Oh! (laughing)
- And they're here.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.545
So everybody, let's say
hi to Harley and Michael.

00:07:21.545 --> 00:07:23.130
(crowd cheering)

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.213
- Oh, my God!

00:07:25.801 --> 00:07:27.516
Stop it.
(crowd cheering)

00:07:27.516 --> 00:07:31.141
They did not tell me you guys were coming.

00:07:31.141 --> 00:07:33.766
- Hello, nice to see
you. I love this green.

00:07:33.766 --> 00:07:35.404
You're beautiful.
- Thank you.

00:07:35.404 --> 00:07:37.230
- Hi. Nice to see you.
- Hi.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.937
- Have a seat. Y'all come have a seat.

00:07:38.937 --> 00:07:41.307
(crowd cheering)

00:07:41.307 --> 00:07:45.843
Oh, my gosh. So this is
the craziest story to me.

00:07:45.843 --> 00:07:49.890
So Harley, so Michael, how
does it feel to be a part

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.750
of something like that,
that you exonerated someone

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.510
that was so, just the injustice
of that, you know, like,

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:55.890
and you're a part of, like,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.260
that freeing of that information?

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.400
- It feels amazing.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.520
I mean, whenever we brought
this case to Michael's dad,

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.347
we really didn't think that
anything would come of it.

00:08:04.347 --> 00:08:06.420
We were so excited when we heard the news

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.460
that he was going to be exonerated.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.860
We sent an email and a
DM to "Crime Junkie."

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.420
We were like, "Y'all did this.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:13.830
Like, I mean, your podcast did this."

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.500
Like, it was-
- No. We told the story.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.930
Like, I had no way of
contacting the chief of police

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.730
or getting him to listen to me at all.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.153
- Okay. Has anybody ever met James?

00:08:22.153 --> 00:08:24.053
- No, we have never met him.

00:08:24.053 --> 00:08:25.080
- No, we haven't gotten to meet him.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.460
- We tried to make it down

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.040
to one of his preliminary hearings.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.650
But we weren't able to make it.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.420
So we're just-
- If he comes out next,

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:34.418
I'm gonna pass out.

00:08:34.418 --> 00:08:35.970
(audience laughing)

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:37.920
- Well, we did reach out to him,

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.900
and he has a message for all of you.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:40.733
So watch this.

00:08:41.724 --> 00:08:43.410
- I'd just like to say thank you

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:48.000
for supporting me in my fight for justice.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.270
It's been a long journey indeed.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.750
But it's finally come to that conclusion

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:59.280
where justice is finally going to prevail.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.880
And supporters like you,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.865
who believe in my innocence

00:09:05.865 --> 00:09:10.865
and have advocated justice

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:14.941
on my behalf, I thank you very, very much.

00:09:14.941 --> 00:09:18.024
(audience clapping)

00:09:19.597 --> 00:09:23.280
- I cannot watch him or listen to him

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.130
and not think, "Oh, my God,
so much life was stolen."

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.030
You know what I'm saying?

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.227
And you are complete
strangers to this human.

00:09:30.227 --> 00:09:31.980
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:34.140
Like, complete strangers
and you ended up helping,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.160
and it's such a beautiful thing.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.560
But also, like, what does
that make you feel like

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.240
hearing that?
- I'm just, like,

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.073
overcome with emotion.

00:09:40.073 --> 00:09:42.360
I mean, and I think the
truth of the matter is, like,

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.830
I think the whole world
would be a better place

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.256
if we stopped acting like we're strangers.

00:09:46.256 --> 00:09:47.250
Like, we're not.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:48.480
- [Kelly] Actually very good point.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:49.380
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.730
It's an incredible thing
what y'all have done.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:51.977
So thank you so much for listening.

00:09:51.977 --> 00:09:53.220
(crowd cheering)

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.970
And thank you so much for acting.

